  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1152 PM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nCurrent radar imagery shows ongoing light showers across the \nforecast area as the upper level shortwave tracks across \nColorado/Wyoming tonight. Mountain snow and rain showers over the \nplains will continue through tonight before exiting the region by \nMonday morning (most precip in Denver should be finished by sunrise, \nwith the northeastern plains still seeing showers in the early \nmorning). In terms of snowfall amounts across the high terrain, \nforecast looks to remain on track, with the northern mountains \nreceiving generally 1-4\" of snow by Monday morning and I-70 Corridor \nand south receiving T-2\". Expect travel impacts across high \nmountain passes for the morning commute. \n\nWith low-level moisture still in place, many people will wake up to \nfog/low clouds in the morning across the Denver metro and plains, \nwith possible impacts to morning commute due to low visibility. \nHowever, expect conditions to gradually improve by 9-10 AM with \nclearing skies in the afternoon. After that, an overall drying trend \nis expected as the upper level shortwave exits the region. We will \nstill be relatively cool on Monday, given the slow morning warm-up, \nwith highs in the upper 50s to low 60s. \n\nRelatively dry and quiet conditions are expected for the forecast \narea Tuesday and through the end of the week, as more zonal flow \naloft returns. An upper level low in the Pacific Northwest is \nprogged to travel east across Idaho and Montana early this week. \nWhile it will stay north of Colorado, it will bring increased \nwesterly flow aloft, resulting in breezy conditions across the high \nterrain Tuesday. Short-range models have been hinting at winds \ngusting up to 40-50 mph periodically in the morning. In terms of \ntemperatures for the week, ensemble solutions are in good agreement \nof above-normal temperatures, with highs in the mid-to-high 60s \nacross the plains. \n\nHigh uncertainty remains in the forecast Friday and into the \nweekend. A weak upper level shortwave will track across the \nsouthwestern United States and into the Central United States. \nHowever, recent global model runs continue to hint that this \ndisturbance will likely be too far south and east to impact our \nforecast area. For now, NBM PoPs of 10-20% seem reasonable at this \ntime for the eastern plains on Friday, but wouldn't be surprised if \nit continues to lower in the coming days. After that, models vary \ndrastically in handling the next upper level low that pushes through \nby the weekend/ early next week. It will likely be able to tap into \ntropical moisture as it tracks east into the Central United States, \nbut there is still high uncertainty which areas will receive the",
